   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to reports on short term\ncrisis respite and intensive crisis respite programs",
    "basePrintNo": "S6486",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6486-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 7,
       "fullName": "Anna Kaplan",
       "imgName": "1044_Anna_Kaplan.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 1224,
       "sessionMemberId": 1222,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "KAPLAN"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §7.09, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6486\n\nSPONSOR: MYRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to\nreports on short term crisis respite and intensive crisis respite\nprograms\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo assess the need statewide for mental health short term and intensive\ncrisis respite programs.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\n*Provides definitions for the purpose of effectuating this act.\n\n*Requires the commissioner of the Office of Mental Health (OMH) to\nfurnish and issue a report to the governor, the speaker of the assembly,\nand temporary president of the senate no later than one year after the\neffectuation of this section on information as it relates to short term\ncrisis respite and intensive crisis respite programs.\n\n*The information in the report includes:\n\n>The number of short term and intensive crisis respite programs in each\ncounty;\n\n>The number of individuals served by short term and intensive crisis\nrespite programs and in each county each month;\n\n>The most prevalent factors for admission of individuals who received\neither type of crisis respite service, including the most prevalent type\nof setting an individual was referred from, and the most prevalent\nmental health diagnosis for individuals accepted into either type of\ncrisis respite program each month;\n\n>The percentage of individuals diverted from the use of inpatient hospi-\ntal emergency room care due to experiencing a mental health crisis, for\nat least thirty days after being discharged from either type of crisis\nrespite program each month;\n\n>Types of services provided by a short term or intensive crisis respite\nprogram, including the type of staff and training required for the\nprovision of such services as well as any additional services or types\nof staff and training that may be beneficial to individuals receiving\nsuch services; and\n\n>Identify parts of the state that would benefit from short term and/or\nintensive crisis respite programs or where an expansion of services\nwould be appropriate\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nWithout crisis respite services, many New Yorkers who suffer from seri-\nous mental illness would have no choice but to check into a hospital or\nan emergency room if they are facing stressors or a calamity in their\npersonal lives. Crisis respite centers cost a fraction of the price in\ncomparison to a psychiatric hospital or emergency room stay, and can be\nfar more effective in supporting individuals to avoid a mental health\ncrisis. In addition, diverting individuals away from inpatient care and\ninto community-based mental health programs such as crisis respite\nservices is particularly vital when the number of beds in New York State\npsychiatric hospitals has declined sharply.\n\nAlthough there is general consensus that crisis respite programs in New\nYork State are effective, more data is needed to determine the most\neffective method for the delivery of short term and intensive crisis\nresidential respite services and whether there is a need for expansion\nin New York State.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nUndetermined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
